  "connections": [],
  "notes": "One clump of pasture grass — the unit a grassland is made of, and the smallest plant the engine carries. Robin, 2026-08-09: \"grass clumps (multiple strands in a clump that can be handled as one unit)\", \"grass is blades, not hexagons or columns\", and \"it's natural for grass to extend beyond the root clump.\" It was a single cylinder and it drew as a hexagonal prism.\n\nNOTHING HERE IS CHOSEN — every number is either measured or solved.\n  * LEAF AREA: the cylinder declared grassland LAI 3.0 over a 0.12 m crown, so one-sided leaf area is LAI x pi R^2 = 0.033929 m^2. N blades of width W and length H then satisfy N x W x H = leaf area, so the COUNT AND THE WIDTH ARE ONE NUMBER, not two.\n  * BLADE WIDTH is the measured one: Lolium perenne (perennial ryegrass — the commonest pasture grass in Ireland, where this is first drawn) has blades 2-3 mm wide (Lucid Centre pasture key; NC State Extension). N = 32 puts the width at 3.03 mm, inside that band. It is the blade that is measured; the count follows.\n  * THE ROOT CLUMP follows from the blades having to FIT around it: N blades of width W need a ring of circumference N x W, so r_root = N x W / 2pi = leaf area / (2 pi H) = 15.43 mm — a number that does not even depend on how the leaf area is divided up.\n  * THE SPLAY is solved, not chosen: sin(theta) = k x sqrt(u), u = i/(N-1), fills the crown by EQUAL AREA, and k = 0.123475 is the value at which the outermost blade reaches exactly R. Blades emerge from a 15.4 mm ring and reach 0.059 m, so the foliage extends well beyond the root clump, as grass does.\n\nMASS IS UNCHANGED BY CONSTRUCTION. The cylinder carried packing 2.571e-03 over a 3.9584e-03 m^3 envelope = 1.017877e-05 m^3 of grass; the blades are solid and total N x W x H x T = 1.017876e-05 m^3. Identical, because both are leaf area x thickness. The clump is the SAME MATTER, resolved — Law IV, the camera changes representation and never existence. Packing is gone with the cylinder: the blades ARE the matter and the air between them is air, which is the distinction Robin drew about sand and sandstone — a packing fraction is a lossy summary of an arrangement.\n\nAzimuth steps by the golden angle, because a tussock is not a regular star.\n\nFLAGGED (Law V, an IOU that names its own fix): a real ryegrass blade ARCHES — the same sources describe a distinct arching keel down the centre of the leaf — and these are STRAIGHT, because a slab is the primitive available. Straightness is also what forces the splay so upright: a 0.35 m straight blade cannot reach far sideways and stay inside a 0.12 m crown, whereas an arching one leans out and drops its tip. The honest version is one part per segment along the arc — 3x the parts and 3x the triangles — and it should follow a measurement of what the arc changes on screen.\n\nAt altitude nobody instantiates a clump: the ground answers with `grass`'s albedo, itself a measured spectrum. This exists for the metre or two where you can see individual plants.",
